Current tests are syntax-only (t/compile-check.sh against the opaque t/ff.h stub) —
they prove the sources compile, not that cdb works. Raise real coverage:

Build an in-memory FatFs shim (concrete FIL over a RAM buffer with real f_read/f_write/
f_lseek), separate from the opaque stub (e.g. under t/mem/, its own ff.h + ff.c, compiled
with -I t/mem). FIL is pointer-only in cdb code, so the shim is clean.

Cover, host-side, without hardware:
  - cdb_make_start/add/finish -> cdb_seek/cdb_bread round-trip, values recovered byte-exact
  - not-found returns 0
  - put modes: ADD, INSERT (dup rejected), REPLACE, WARN, REPLACE0
  - cdb_hash / cdb_pack / cdb_unpack round-trip
  - an error path: force a failing f_lseek and assert it propagates (ties to task k1)

Add a runner (t/run-tests.sh) and wire it into .github/workflows alongside compile-check.
Optionally a gcov target to report line coverage. Never vendor real Chan FatFs into t/.
Lane: tinycdbff-test-writer (intent owned by the dispatcher).

